-- title:
--   Frobenius parity of the leg of Xi over connected bases
-- statement:
--   Fix primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$ and a positive integer $N$ not divisible by $r$. The arithmetic input consists of: a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ that is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the $\pi$-adic topology for an irreducible $\pi$, with residue ring of cardinality $r$ and $(r)=(\pi)$, together with its fraction field $K_0$; and a characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element integral over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$. The geometric input consists of: rationals $a,b$ with $(0<a$ or $0<b)$ and such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the places above $r$ and $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ containing all rational integers; a coordinate map $\mathrm{coord}:\Lambda\to \mathbb Z_{r^2}\times\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ satisfying the additivity, unitality, twisted multiplicativity, injectivity, density and trace conditions of `IsOrderCoord`; a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ with formal coordinates $\theta_0$ in two variables exhibiting it as a formal module over a height-$4$ formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$; a ring map $\iota:\mathbb Z_{r^2}\to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ and a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ of height $4$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$; a moduli package $M$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is a Zariski sheaf, together with a comparison $\eta$ from rigidified objects of $\Phi$ to points of $M$ that separates admissible rigidified objects exactly up to isomorphism, is natural in ring maps, and is surjective after passing to a distinguished affine cover; an injective embedding $E_0$ of the centraliser of the $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$-action and of $\varpi$ on $\Phi$ into $2\times2$ matrices over $K_0$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ with scaling exponent $r^{m}$; a ring map $\kappa:O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)\to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps; and an isogeny $\beta_0$ of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$. Finally, $\Xi$ assigns to each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, each $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi:O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, and each rigidified fake elliptic curve over $(B,\psi)$ a $G$-point of $M$, subject to: the leg clause, that over a base with no idempotents other than $0,1$ the coefficient leg of $\Xi$ is $\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{k}$ for some $k\in\mathbb Z$; naturality under pullback of rigidified curves along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves compatible with the isogeny legs up to powers of $r$; and the defining clause, that over a base with no nontrivial idempotents and with $r$ nilpotent a rigidified curve admitting global formal coordinates $\theta$ exhibiting it as a formal module over some $X$ has $\Xi$-value $\langle \psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-j},\,\eta(t)\rangle$ for some $j\le 1$ and some rigidified $t$ with $t.X = X$ that is the rigid transport of $\theta$ through $\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0$ and is admissible for $\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$. The conclusion is that for every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, every $\psi$, every proof that $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B$, every $B$ whose idempotents are only $0$ and $1$, and every rigidified fake elliptic curve $x$ over $(B,\psi)$, there is $j\in\{0,1\}$ with $(\Xi\,x).\psi = \psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$; no global formal coordinates on $x$ are assumed.
--
--   This sharpens the leg clause of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison $\Xi$ from an unspecified integral Frobenius twist to one of the two twists $\mathrm{Fr}^{0}$, $\mathrm{Fr}^{-1}$, the parity reflecting that $\mathcal O_D$-isogenies of special formal modules have even height while relative Frobenius has height $2$. It is used in the subsequent construction of rigidifications with prescribed Frobenius leg and in the descent of $\Xi$ along distinguished affine covers.
